  "versions": [
    {
      "meaning": "Divine order and completeness",
      "description": "The number ten represents divine order and completeness in governance. God gave the Ten Commandments as the foundation for His people's law. The ten plagues struck Egypt. The tithe (one-tenth) was given back to God as recognition of His sovereignty. The ten virgins in Jesus' parable represent the fullness of those waiting.",
      "reason": "[minor] The description is good and comprehensive but lacks some important biblical references that could strengthen the text, such as ten generations from Adam to Noah, or the requirement of ten men for a minyan. Some of these fit better as footnotes to enrich the content without breaking the flow.; [minor] The meaning field is precise but could also include 'governance' or 'legislation,' since the Ten Commandments are the most central example and deal specifically with divine law and governance.; [minor] There are interesting facts related to the number 10 that work well as footnotes: the minyan requirement in Jewish tradition, the ten sefirot in Kabbalah, and the ten generations from Adam to Noah and from Noah to Abraham.",
      "score": 7,
      "date": "2026-04-04"
    }
  ],
  "footnotes": [
    {
      "text": "In Jewish tradition, at least ten adult men (minyan) are required to hold a complete worship service, which underscores the significance of the number ten as an expression of the community's completeness.",
      "source": "rabbinsk"
    },
    {
      "text": "In Kabbalah, there are ten sefirot (divine emanations) that make up the Tree of Life and represent God's complete way of revealing Himself to the world.",
      "source": "kabbalistisk"
    },
    {
      "text": "Genesis lists ten generations from Adam to Noah (Gen 5) and ten generations from Noah to Abraham (Gen 11), marking complete epochs in salvation history.",
      "source": "teologisk"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Hebrew numeral system uses the letter yod (י) for the number 10. Yod is the smallest letter in the Hebrew alphabet, but also the first letter of God's name (YHWH), symbolizing that the divine exists even in what appears small.",
      "source": "lingvistisk"
    }
  ]
